[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-26", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 26 — full SF1 passes the gate and is refused as a row: the SUT pages at ~530 B per quad", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-26.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 26 - full SF1 passes the gate and is refused as a row: the SUT pages at ~530 B per quad on 16 GiB\n\n> Seed: iteration 25's S2' (the SF1-proper row) and S16 (the gate's cost).\n> Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung SF1 proper; origin :cross-host,\n> path :lan.\n\n## Generate\n\n  S16 first: the 30-minute gate was the naive IC09 twin. `by-avet` now\n  reads arrangement.core/by-predicate-value (a set of subjects; the\n  `eng/datoms` route built a wire-shaped map per subject) and `top-k-by`\n  keeps a bounded 20 under the LDBC tie rule instead of sorting 80k\n  candidates. In-process on the 35k subset, two highest-degree persons\n  (fof 7-8k, 75-80k candidates): 1,081 / 897 -> 218 / 217 ms. Same\n  answers on the 3,500-post gate (846/846).\n\n  Then the rung. The first restart (13 GB max, 3 GB young) sat with the\n  old generation 100% full after a forced GC and 295% CPU in GC -- the\n  live set is larger than 9 GB. Restarted at -Xmx14500m -Xmn1g.\n\n  Prediction: the gate passes with the IC09 fix; a resident SUT gives an\n  SF1-proper row with the empty share near zero; memory is the risk.\n\n## Rank\n\n  Gate: 1082 checked, 0 disagreements -- inside the load wait with the\n  new twins; the iteration 25 fix holds on the rung that found it.\n\n  Memory (SUT-reported): used heap 10,954 MB, RSS 8,476 MB; the host had\n  created an 8 GB swap file (total 0 all day) with 6.6 GB used, 1.0M\n  pages in the compressor, 3.8M page-ins. ~530 B per quad on this shape\n  -- the 337 B of the 2M-quad subset does not extrapolate; 3.06M content\n  strings and 3M-entry index maps weigh more per quad than reified knows.\n\n  Probe (levi, 11-op mix, official parameters):\n    rate   on-time%  thru/s  empty  ic02   ic07  ic08  ic09  is2   is3\n     64    100.00     63.7   7.7%   111.0  29.4  4.0   86.1  25.1  10.9\n    256     20.08    201.1\n\n  Against the resident subset (ic02 3.4, ic09 5.6, is2 2.8): a paging\n  SUT measured through paging -- iteration 14's shape, on the SUT side.\n\n  The driver's residency rule did not say so: swap 80.7% < 90 and RSS\n  0.77 x heap > 0.5. Now RSS below 0.95 x used heap refuses (a resident\n  JVM's RSS exceeds its used heap); control: this SUT is refused\n  (8,259 < 11,014).\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Prediction half held: the gate and the fix, yes; the row, no. The\n    empty share fell from 33-43% to 7.7% -- SF1 proper is where the\n    official parameters mean what LDBC meant -- and that is exactly the\n    rung this host cannot hold resident.\n  - Two footprint numbers now stand side by side, both measured: 337 B\n    (2M quads, subset) and ~530 B (20.6M, full). Neither is \"the\" number;\n    the shape of the data is in it.\n  - The judge learned a rule from a near miss, again: thresholds set from\n    one incident (0.5 x heap from iteration 14's 0.02 vs 0.9) are the\n    incident's numbers, not the property's. \"RSS below used heap\" is the\n    property.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict: REFUSED-HOST-MEMORY (no row at SF1 proper). M3's wall is named\nwith a number: this heap shape needs ~24 GB free for SF1, and SF30 is\n30x that. The 35k SUT is back on simeon.\n\nSeeds for iteration 27, in order:\n\n  - S17 (footprint): where the 530 B go -- content strings (interning\n    repeats; off-heap; or not loading `message/content` until IS4 asks),\n    boxed longs as strings, four persistent index maps. Measure by\n    attribute before touching anything.\n  - S18 (host): a 32 GB machine on the fleet, or this workstation once\n    it is not paging, is the cheapest way to an SF1-proper row.\n  - S15 (coverage): IC01/IC03... need static tables (tags, places,\n    universities); each is more quads, which S17 has to pay for first.\n  - M2 (updates) stays the design step.\n"}]
